{
  "requirement": "Rendering the view of\n{cite}`requirements.test-coverage.index.file_map.notes` MUST NOT invoke an\nassessment model. It MUST follow\n{cite}`requirements.test-coverage.index.render_only`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations derived from the subject**\n1. Rendering the file-notes view MUST NOT invoke an assessment model.\n2. The render MUST follow `requirements.test-coverage.index.render_only`. That means it reads source annotations and stored review results only. It must not read saved execution measurements, evaluate exception acceptance, or audit review freshness. The cited `requirements.index.render_only` is context only.\n\n**Implementation shape**\nI read `src/execution_coverage/notes.rs#fn[pages]`, which carries the `{impl}` reference. Its signature takes only a root, a file set and an index path. It parses source, collects `habeas:coverage` citations and renders their explanations. It has no model or assessment hook. The only observable ways a model call could show up are a new or updated review file, a created `target` directory, or a re-assessed or flagged-stale display. The tests check each of these.\n\n**Test mapping**\n- `src/execution_coverage/notes.rs#mod[tests]/fn[each_render_reads_current_notes_and_keeps_recorded_reviews_without_writing]`:\n  - It writes a stored review whose hash is stale, because the review is written before the source changes. It then edits the source and renders again.\n  - It asserts that the page shows the updated explanation and still shows \"Recorded review: **Recorded true**\".\n  - It asserts that \"citations changed\" does not appear, which rules out a freshness audit or re-assessment.\n  - It asserts that the review file bytes are unchanged and that no `target` directory exists. A model-authored review would have changed the file or created `target`.\n- `src/execution_coverage/book.rs#mod[tests]/fn[book_ignores_saved_measurements_and_preserves_artifacts]`:\n  - It populates the full book, including the \"File notes: code.rs\" chapter, with a missing review. The first render asserts that no `target` exists.\n  - It then adds a saved coverage attempt containing a distinctive test name. The rendered chapters are identical to those from before the attempt, and the name appears in none of them. This covers not reading saved measurements.\n  - It corrupts the attempt metadata and checks that the output is unchanged, that the artifacts are preserved, and that no selection cache is written.\n- `src/execution_coverage/book.rs#mod[tests]/fn[overview_shows_missing_unreadable_and_recorded_reviews_with_current_justifications]`:\n  - It renders with missing, true, false, FIXME, invalid and unreadable stored reviews.\n  - It asserts that only the stored state is displayed. \"current and accepted\" and \"input is stale\" never appear, so there is no acceptance evaluation or freshness audit.\n  - It changes the source under a stored review and asserts that the review bytes are unchanged and that no `target` directory exists.\n\n**Gaps considered**\nNo test can directly observe \"no model was called\", because the render path has no injectable model seam. The tests rule out the observable effects of a model call, so I count this as adequate coverage rather than a gap. The scenarios that matter (missing, stale and stored reviews, saved measurements present, and write and side-effect checks) are all exercised with assertions. I found no unacknowledged gap.",
